A||||2||Steven||Trans Seals.||||1||
Z||000000||Steven||07-16-2001||05:38 PM||||Well I am starting to leave little spots wherever I park. Started looking around under the car, and it is coming from the trans seals where the drive shafts go into the trans. Who has changed these seals (with the trans and engine in the car)? Any little problems you ran into? Any thing you would do differently (besides paying someone else to do it for you)? Please pass along any info please. My car is an '85 GT w/4sp manual trans.||||reg||1
Z||000001||starfighter007@msn.com||07-16-2001||06:01 PM||||Thats not to hard of ajob to change those axel sealsauto zone carrys them for acouple bucks a piece you will have to pop the axels out of the trans.it is kinda straight forward with a few basic tools and a small prybar.I dont think I would try this myself if yor not very mech.inclined.but if you were to do it I would guess it would take an average person with basic skills in the drive way maybe about 4 to 5 hours some where in that time frame to do and of course we are here to help you along with any caveats to watch for if you do decide to do it let us know what you want to do.oh and get your self a haynes manual has good info.look forward to hearing from you||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||07-16-2001||07:45 PM||||Make sure when you pry the old on out that you don't damage the tranny opening. If you do, paint opening with Permatex Super 300 sealer for small scratches. Use RTV Silicone if it's deaper but don't get sloppy. Waste Silicone can leave particles that could block something up.||||reg||1
